\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first systematic characterization of how multi-class annotation errors interact with causal DAG topology. Our bias taxonomy across seven topologies reveals that the classical binary guarantee of bias toward the null breaks for $K \geq 3$: exposure and IV topologies can produce sign reversal (83.4\% and 52.6\% respectively), while five topologies maintain a structural guarantee of no sign flip. The ASV framework provides topology-aware sensitivity diagnostics, revealing a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile) to M-bias (immune). Our correction guidance analysis reveals that correction method effectiveness is itself topology-dependent: a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance result proves matrix-based corrections are ineffective for four topologies, while PPI++ achieves 93.8\% bias reduction at the cost of gold-standard labels. Binarization collapse analysis further demonstrates that simplifying multi-class annotations to binary introduces additional bias varying 147$\times$ across topologies.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our framework assumes non-differential misclassification: $P(A^*|A, Y, \mathbf{Z}) = P(A^*|A)$. This holds when the LLM annotator has no access to the outcome $Y$, but may be violated when few-shot examples are selected conditional on $Y$ or when annotation difficulty varies with the outcome. Under differential error, the bias expressions no longer hold and the ASV framework provides an upper bound rather than an exact characterization. Closed-form bias expressions are derived for $K{=}3$ and verified numerically at $K{=}5,7$; expressions for arbitrary $K$ remain open. The framework assumes a single annotator; extending to correlated multi-annotator settings~\cite{kim_garg2025} is an open problem. The empirical validation spans $K{=}2,3,10$ with four LLMs, confirming the theoretical bias ranking, sign-flip guarantees, and $F_1$ disconnect across all $K$; however, VAST and CivilComments use a 7B model rather than frontier models, limiting direct cross-tier comparisons.

\paragraph{Future work.}
Immediate directions include extending to differential misclassification and incorporating correlated multi-LLM error structures into correction strategies. We release an open-source Python toolkit (\texttt{annot-sensitivity}) that computes ASVs from estimated confusion matrices and routes to topology-appropriate correction methods.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

The consequences can be catastrophic. Under $K{=}3$ non-differential exposure misclassification, 83.4\% of confusion matrices cause \emph{sign reversal} of the estimated treatment effect, where the estimated direction of the causal relationship is opposite to the truth. This failure breaks the classical binary guarantee that non-differential misclassification biases estimates ``toward the null''~\cite{brenner1993}, a guarantee that many applied researchers implicitly rely upon. The breakdown is not an edge case: it persists even when classifier accuracy exceeds 70\% (13.7\% violation rate at diagonal $\geq 0.7$). Moreover, standard classification metrics do not predict downstream causal risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%), illustrating that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk.

The mechanism is algebraic. For binary classification, non-differential error can only attenuate the effect because the $2{\times}2$ stochastic confusion matrix preserves coefficient signs. With $K \geq 3$ categories, the confusion matrix can assign negative effective weights to categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{brenner1993} identified the possibility of polytomous reversal but did not characterize it across causal designs. \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology (unobserved confounding). No existing framework addresses how the same confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across different causal identification strategies.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification (closed-form for confounding, exposure, and mediation; numerically validated for all seven). We establish that five topologies maintain a structural sign-flip guarantee (with formal proofs for three topologies and Monte Carlo validation for all seven), while exposure and IV topologies can produce sign reversal. This taxonomy reveals that bias direction is determined by the \emph{interaction} between the confusion matrix and the DAG topology, not by either alone. The taxonomy extends to binarization: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying up to 147$\times$ across topologies.

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs).} We propose a unified sensitivity analysis framework with three complementary measures: magnitude ASV (E-value analogue for annotation error), significance ASV (hypothesis test filter for attenuating topologies), and sign-flip ASV (for exposure and IV). ASVs reveal a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile, 52.6\% sign reversal; sign-flip ASV $= 1.1$--$6.7$) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$), providing practitioners with topology-aware robustness diagnostics.

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods across all seven topologies and find a negative result with significant practical implications that, while theoretically predicted by Frisch--Waugh--Lovell, has been overlooked in the annotation correction literature: matrix-based corrections (naive plug-in, MLA) achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four of seven topologies, because the column space of the design matrix is invariant to invertible confusion-matrix transformations. Among gold-free methods, only MC-SIMEX provides positive bias reduction (20.9\% grand mean). PPI++ achieves 93.8\% bias reduction with nominal coverage but requires gold-standard labels.
\end{enumerate}

